.TH GIT-@-LOGZ 1 "August 2025" "GitAT v1.1.0" "Git Workflow Management Tool"
.SH NAME
git-@-logz \- View commit history with enhanced formatting
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git @ logz
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBlogz\fR command provides a nicely formatted view of the commit history. It's a GitAT-enhanced version of 'git log' with better output formatting that follows the GitAT styling conventions.
.PP
The command was renamed from the original \fBlogs\fR command to avoid conflicts with standard Git commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.PP
All standard 'git log' options are supported. Some common ones:
.TP
.B -n, --max-count=<number>
Limit the number of commits to output
.TP
.B --oneline
Show each commit as a single line
.TP
.B --graph
Draw a text-based graphical representation of the commit history
.TP
.B --all
Show all branches
.TP
.B --author=<pattern>
Show only commits by authors matching the pattern
.TP
.B -h, --help
Show help message
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Show last 10 commits (default):
.RS
.nf
git @ logz
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show last 5 commits:
.RS
.nf
git @ logz -5
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show commits with graph:
.RS
.nf
git @ logz --graph --oneline
.fi
.RE
.PP
Show all commits by a specific author:
.RS
.nf
git @ logz --author="John Doe"
.fi
.RE
.SH DEFAULT BEHAVIOR
.PP
By default, the command shows the last 10 commits with oneline formatting and GitAT styling.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
.PP
The command formats output with GitAT's colorful logging system, making it easier to read than standard git log output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
.BR git-@ (1),
.BR git-@-shasum (1),
.BR git (1)